## 7. Risk Assessment

### 7.1 Known Unknowns (Explicitly Flagged)

| Risk | Source | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| **DAG comprehension timeline** | No user testing exists for DAG-as-portfolio-metaphor | DAG explanatory label included (~2–4 s reading time). Treat DAG as optional differentiator, not core requirement. |
| **Orange → trust pathway** | Color-tool associations are factually correct; causal trust increase is untested hypothesis | Any tool-associated accent color works. If testing shows no trust effect, swap to blue (K8s/Databricks) or red (TensorFlow/VS Code). |
| **Edge connection visibility** | Viewers may not notice SVG lines between sections | Edge rendering is a marginal addition (< 2 KB JS). If user testing shows no effect, remove without architectural impact. |
| **Portfolio vs. resume screening time** | Beiler (2022) studies resume screening, not portfolio screening. Portfolios may receive more or less time. | Design is optimized for fast scanning (headings, tags, scannable content) — works for both short and long windows. |
| **Generative variation** | Zero effect at first visit (the primary decision point) | **Excluded** from Tier 1. Only consider for Tier 2 if repeat-visit analytics justify the complexity. |

### 7.2 Risk Matrix

| Risk | Probability | Impact | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|---|
| DAG metaphor confuses more viewers than it impresses | Medium | High (early exit at pre-attentive phase) | Collapse to linear layout on mobile; label explains metaphor; A/B test with and without DAG encoding |
| Viewer exits before reading project content | Low (with visual coherence) | Critical (content is the causal bottleneck) | Clear headings + scannable tags ensure content identification within 5–10 s |
| Monospace labels perceived as "code aesthetic" rather than "engineering precision" | Low | Low (label effect is small) | Only use monospace for technical tags (PyTorch, Kubernetes) and depth badges |
| Color blindness: orange on dark background is low contrast | Medium | Medium | Orange accent has `--accent-dim` variant that provides sufficient contrast against `--bg-primary`. All text uses `--text-primary` which passes WCAG AA. |

